  "original_html": "<section aria-labelledby=\"primary-heading\" class=\"flex w-full flex-1 flex-col h-full max-w-[1024px]\"><h1 id=\"primary-heading\" class=\"sr-only\">Home</h1><div><div class=\"max-w-[680px] m-auto px-1 flex flex-col gap-y-6\"><h1 class=\"text-4xl font-bold\">Teacher as Researcher: Try, Reflect, Share</h1><div class=\"border-b border-slate-200\"></div><div id=\"pm-1\" class=\"border-0 flex flex-col gap-y-4 ProseMirror\" data-context=\"doc\" data-input-id=\"pm-doc-html-63533ecf-0795-4660-99b2-e55235e51946\" data-editable=\"false\" data-progress=\"\" contenteditable=\"false\" translate=\"yes\"><h2 class=\"text-4xl font-bold\" data-version=\"0\" id=\"h_-framing-our-work\">🖼️ Framing Our Work</h2><p>In the fall, you stepped into teacher-research by trying a strategy, observing what happened, and reflecting on what you learned. At its core, teacher-research assumes something we know from experience: the daily work of classrooms generates powerful insights when we slow down, pay attention, and ask curious questions about our students and our teaching.</p><p>This spring, we build on that foundation through a shared inquiry. Rather than selecting different strategies, all teachers will teach the <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Een_v-cS36h8S-FMilGiDar9ntBV7i9Y99cfHcM5R_o/edit?usp=sharing\" class=\"underline\" target=\"_blank\"><span><b>Layered Writing lesson sequence</b></span></a>, with their choice of text set topic. Because everyone will be engaging with the same lesson sequence, we can look across our classrooms to notice patterns in student thinking, identify common challenges, and deepen our understanding of how layered reading and writing support argument development.</p><p>The goal here is to teach the layered writing process and observe closely: <i>How do students build and revise their thinking as they encounter new perspectives? What surprises you? What questions arise? What does this reveal about their literacy skills?</i> Your reflections will not only support your Module 4 work, but will also prepare you for our <b>spring in-person regroup on April 9th</b>, where you’ll bring a small set of student papers from this sequence so we can analyze student work together.</p><h2 class=\"text-4xl font-bold\" data-version=\"0\" id=\"h_-what-to-expect\">📝What to Expect</h2><p>Although our approach is different, the discussions will work just as they did in the fall: one for preparing to teach, one for reflecting during teaching, and one for looking back afterward. You’ll participate in all three discussions to earn the discussion badge for this module.</p><h3 class=\"text-2xl font-semibold\" id=\"h_-what-to-expect__h_in-this-module-you-will\">In this module, you will:</h3><p>✅ <b>Step into the role of teacher-researcher</b> by teaching the Layered Writing lesson sequence and paying close attention to how students build, revise, and articulate their thinking.</p><p>✅ <b>Participate in a cycle of three connected discussions</b>: preparing to teach, reflecting during teaching (including sharing artifacts), and reflecting after teaching.</p><p>✅ <b>Share your observations, artifacts, and reflections</b> with other teachers to notice patterns across classrooms and learn from one another’s experiences.</p><p>✅ <b>Earn the Teacher as Researcher microcredential</b> by connecting with your facilitator to reflect more deeply on what you learned, upload an artifact or anecdote, and identify next steps or new questions for your teaching.</p><div data-type=\"doc-embed\" data-doc-id=\"de1e02b9-5f64-438f-a472-0021135e9a92\" data-doc-type=\"page\" contenteditable=\"false\"><a href=\"https://participate.community/docs/de1e02b9-5f64-438f-a472-0021135e9a92\" class=\"flex flex-col border border-slate-300\"><div class=\"font-bold bg-slate-300 h-6 w-full pl-4\">Page</div><div class=\"flex justify-between\"><div class=\"p-4 flex flex-col justify-between\"><p class=\"font-bold line-clamp-1\">Preparing to Teach | Spring 202